To create a custom warning for unsaved changes before leaving a webpage, you can utilize the `window.onbeforeunload` event in JavaScript. However, it's important to note that you cannot fully customize the dialog that appears; you can only append a message to the default dialog.

Here’s a basic implementation:

1. **Track the "dirty" state of your form**: You need a function to determine if the form has unsaved changes. This can be done by comparing the current values of the form elements with their default values.

   ```javascript
   function formIsDirty(form) {
       for (var i = 0; i < form.elements.length; i++) {
           var element = form.elements[i];
           var type = element.type;
           if (type === "checkbox" || type === "radio") {
               if (element.checked !== element.defaultChecked) {
                   return true;
               }
           } else if (["hidden", "password", "text", "textarea"].includes(type)) {
               if (element.value !== element.defaultValue) {
                   return true;
               }
           } else if (["select-one", "select-multiple"].includes(type)) {
               for (var j = 0; j < element.options.length; j++) {
                   if (element.options[j].selected !== element.options[j].defaultSelected) {
                       return true;
                   }
               }
           }
       }
       return false;
   }
   ```

2. **Set up the `onbeforeunload` event**: You can then set up the `onbeforeunload` event to trigger when the user attempts to leave the page.

   ```javascript
   window.onbeforeunload = function(e) {
       e = e || window.event;
       if (formIsDirty(document.forms["yourFormName"])) {
           var message = "You have unsaved changes!";
           // For IE and Firefox
           if (e) {
               e.returnValue = message;
           }
           // For Safari
           return message;
       }
   };
   ```

Replace `"yourFormName"` with the actual name of your form. This code will prompt the user with a warning if they try to navigate away with unsaved changes.

Keep in mind that while you can set a custom message, the browser will still display its own default message along with yours, and you cannot fully customize the dialog box itself [How can I override the OnBeforeUnload dialog and replace it with my own?](276660). 

For a more comprehensive solution, consider using libraries that handle form state management and unsaved changes, which can simplify the implementation [Client/JS Framework for "Unsaved Data" Protection?](140460).

