To stop fetching external definitions, such as DTDs, while processing an XML document in Perl, you can use the `XML::Parser` module with the `NoLWP` option set to `1`. This prevents the parser from using LWP to resolve external entities. You can then pass this parser to `XML::XPath`. Here’s how you can do it:

```perl
use XML::XPath;
use XML::Parser;

my $p = XML::Parser->new(NoLWP => 1);
my $xp = XML::XPath->new(parser => $p, filename => "a.xhtml");
```

Keep in mind that by doing this, you will lose all entities except for numerical ones and the default ones (like `>`, `<`, `&`, `'`, and `"`). If you are looking for a more robust solution, consider using `XML::LibXML`, which is more powerful and faster than `XML::XPath` and does not have this issue with external definitions [How can I prevent XML::XPath from fetching a DTD while processing an XML file?](303510).

To create a query string from a list of field names in Java, you can use a method similar to the one described in the context. You can iterate over a map of field names and their corresponding values, encoding them appropriately to form a query string. Here's a sample implementation based on the provided context:

```java
import java.io.UnsupportedEncodingException;
import java.net.URLEncoder;
import java.util.Map;

public String toQueryString(Map<String, String> data) throws UnsupportedEncodingException {
    StringBuilder queryString = new StringBuilder();
    for (Map.Entry<String, String> pair : data.entrySet()) {
        queryString.append(URLEncoder.encode(pair.getKey(), "UTF-8"))
                   .append("=")
                   .append(URLEncoder.encode(pair.getValue(), "UTF-8"))
                   .append("&");
    }
    if (queryString.length() > 0) {
        queryString.deleteCharAt(queryString.length() - 1); // Remove the last '&'
    }
    return queryString.toString();
}
```

This method constructs a query string by encoding each key-value pair in the map and appending them to a `StringBuilder`. Finally, it removes the trailing '&' character before returning the complete query string. This approach is similar to the `http_build_query` function in PHP, as mentioned in the context [Is there any Java equivalent of PHP's http_build_query function?](636770).
